    "f8850bb1-4cfb-5bc7-a066-0087aabd2028": {
      "type": "StorySegmentLayer",
      "name": "2. Why Delhi, why November",
      "visible": true,
      "parameters": {
        "zoom": 5.14,
        "extent": [
          5641671.79,
          445640.11,
          15437786.98,
          4579425.81
        ],
        "enableIdentify": true,
        "content": {
          "contentMode": "map",
          "markdown": "<div style='font-size:16px; line-height:1.5; min-height:84vh;'><div style='font-size:.8em; color:#64748b; letter-spacing:.03em; margin:0 0 .5em;'>1 · Background</div><ul style='margin:0 0 .5em 1.1em; padding:0;'><li style='margin:0 0 .35em 0;'>Delhi-NCR suffers some of the world's worst particulate pollution from late October to January: stubble burning, Diwali fireworks, traffic, construction and power plants.</li><li style='margin:0 0 .35em 0;'>On winter nights the <b>mixing layer (the layer of air pollution mixes into) drops to 100–300 m and winds die down</b>, trapping pollution near the ground. Same emissions, worse nights.</li><li style='margin:0 0 .35em 0;'>India already has the institutions: a national AQI (CPCB) and the staged GRAP measures (construction bans, vehicle limits, online classes…).</li></ul><div style='background:#f8fafc; border:1px solid #cbd5e1; padding:.45em .7em; border-radius:8px; margin:.5em 0; font-size:.9em; color:#1e293b;'>The question is not \"how bad\" but <b>\"when, where, why, and what should citizens do\"</b>, 12 to 48 hours ahead. The square on the map is the Delhi region this story covers.</div></div>",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": "",
          "paneAlignment": "end",
          "panelWidth": "34%"
        },
        "transition": {
          "type": "linear",
          "time": 1.5
        },
        "layerOverride": [
          {
            "targetLayer": "ee2531a6-23fa-5db3-8289-12a808582c05",
            "visible": false
